arseval frame in $\\R^n$. If such a scaling exists, $F$ is said to be scalable. A scaling $c$ is a minimal scaling if $\\{f_i : c(i)>0\\}$ has no proper scalable subframe. It is known that the set of all scalings of $F$ is a convex polytope whose vertices correspond to minimal scalings. In this paper, we provide an estimation of the number of minimal scalings of a scalable frame and a characterization of when minimal scalings are affinely dependent. Using this characterization, we can conclude that all strict scalings $c=(c(1),\\dots,c(k))\\in \\R_{> 0}^k$ of $F$ have the same structural property. We also present the uniqueness of orthogonal partitioning property of any set of minimal scalings, which provides all possible tight subframes of a given scaled frame.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimal scalings and structural properties of scalable frames (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/280912035_Minimal_scalings_and_structural_properties_of_scalable_frames)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Minimal scalings and structural properties of scalable frames ... In this paper, we provide an estimation of the number of minimal scalings of a scalable frame and a characterization of when minimal scalings are affinely dependent.\n\n# Minimal scalings and structural properties of scalable frames\n\n  * August 2015\n  * Operators and Matrices 11(4)\n\nDOI:10.7153/oam-2017-11-73\n\nAuthors:\n\nAlice Chan\n\nAlice Chan\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nRachel Domagalski\n\nRachel Domagalski\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nYeon Hyang Kim\n\nYeon Hyang Kim\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nSivaram K. Narayan\n\n  * Central Michigan University\n\nShow all 6 authors Hide\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a unit-norm frame $F = \\{f_i\\}_{i=1}^k$ in $\\R^n$, a scaling is a vector $c=(c(1),\\dots,c(k))\\in \\R_{\\geq 0}^k$ such that $\\{\\sqrt{c(i)}f_i\\}_{i =1}^k$ is a Parseval frame in $\\R^n$. If such a scaling exists, F is said to be scalable. A scaling c is a minimal scaling if $\\{f_i : c(i)>0\\}$ has no proper scalable subframe. It is known that the set of all scalings of F is a convex polytope whose vertices correspond to minimal scalings. In this paper, we provide an estimation of the number of minimal scalings of a scalable frame and a characterization of when minimal scalings are affinely dependent. Using this characterization, we can conclude that all strict scalings $c=(c(1),\\dots,c(k))\\in \\R_{> 0}^k$ of F have the same structural property. We also present the uniqueness of orthogonal partitioning property of any set of minimal scalings, which provides all possible tight subframes of a given scaled frame.\n\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\n## Citations (10)\n\n## References (21)\n\n... Remark 9. If F n×(k−s) has full row rank in the above theorem, we obtain G n×(k−s) using the pseudoinverse formula from Theorem 4, which is the minimum norm solution for the equation (4). We note that if H = 0, then the pseudoinverse formula provides the canonical dual frame of F . ...\n\n... By introducing scaling of the vectors in H (see [4,5,7]), we find a dual frame for F : ...\n\nDual Frame Completion Problem\n\nPreprint\n\n  * Jan 2025\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinimal scalings and structural properties of scalable frames - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Minimal_scalings_and_structural_properties_of_scalable_frames)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim